>

  <MedicalNote />

  <ArticleImage src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517836357463-d25dfeac3438?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80" alt="High protein meal prep with chicken, eggs, and healthy vegetables" />

  <div class="not-prose mb-8 grid gap-4 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-5 sm:p-6 shadow-sm">
    <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-2">The Golden Rule of Macros</h2>
    <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">1 gram of protein = <span class="text-brand">4 calories</span></p>
    <p class="text-sm text-body mt-2">This is the universal scientific measurement. To find out how many calories are coming from protein in your diet, simply multiply the total grams of protein by 4.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-science-of-protein-calories">The Science of Protein Calories: The Atwater System</h2>
  <p>If you've ever tracked your macros or flipped over a nutrition label, you've likely noticed that the math is incredibly consistent. That consistency is thanks to the <strong>Atwater System</strong>, developed in the late 19th century by Wilbur Olin Atwater.</p>
  <p>Through rigorous scientific testing using a bomb calorimeter—a device that measures the heat released when food is literally burned to ash—Atwater established the foundational energy values for human macronutrients. He discovered that while fat is highly energy-dense, protein and carbohydrates provide identical raw caloric energy per gram:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protein:</strong> 4 kcal per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> 4 kcal per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Fats:</strong> 9 kcal per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Alcohol (Ethanol):</strong> 7 kcal per gram</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Whether you are eating a prime rib steak, a block of tofu, or drinking a whey protein shake, the protein molecules (amino acids) in those foods will always deliver 4 calories of gross energy per gram to your body.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-calculate-protein">How to Calculate Protein Calories (Conversion Formulas)</h2>
  <p>Converting back and forth between grams and calories is a crucial skill for flexible dieting, meal prepping, and tracking your daily intake. Here is the simple math:</p>

  <div class="not-prose grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline p-5 shadow-sm bg-white">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold tracking-wider text-mute uppercase mb-2">Grams to Calories</p>
      <p class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">Grams × 4 = Calories</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-body mt-2"><strong>Example:</strong> If you eat 150g of protein today, you are getting 600 calories strictly from protein.<br/><em>(150 × 4 = 600)</em></p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline p-5 shadow-sm bg-white">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold tracking-wider text-mute uppercase mb-2">Calories to Grams</p>
      <p class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">Calories ÷ 4 = Grams</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-body mt-2"><strong>Example:</strong> If you want exactly 800 calories of your diet to be protein, you need to eat 200g of protein.<br/><em>(800 ÷ 4 = 200)</em></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="protein-calorie-chart">Quick Reference: Protein Calorie Chart</h2>
  <p>If you don't want to do the math in your head, use this quick reference table to see how protein grams translate into calories, and what percentage of a standard 2,000-calorie diet they represent.</p>
  
